## Proof

**Yes**, it is possible to construct a discontinuous function $f:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$ satisfying $f(x+y)=f(x)+f(y)$ for all $x,y\in\mathbb{R}$.

### Construction via a Hamel basis

**Step 1: $\mathbb{R}$ as a vector space over $\mathbb{Q}$.**

View $\mathbb{R}$ as a vector space over the field $\mathbb{Q}$. By the Axiom of Choice (applied via Zorn's Lemma), every vector space has a basis. So there exists a **Hamel basis** $B\subset\mathbb{R}$ of $\mathbb{R}$ over $\mathbb{Q}$: a linearly independent (over $\mathbb{Q}$) set such that every real number $x$ can be written **uniquely** as

$$x = q_1 b_1 + q_2 b_2 + \cdots + q_n b_n,$$

where $q_i\in\mathbb{Q}$, $b_i\in B$, and $n\in\mathbb{N}$.

**Step 2: Define $f$ on the basis, then extend $\mathbb{Q}$-linearly.**

Pick any basis element $b_0\in B$ and define $f$ on $B$ by:

$$f(b) = \begin{cases} 1 & \text{if } b = b_0, \\ 0 & \text{if } b \neq b_0. \end{cases}$$

For an arbitrary $x = \sum_{i=1}^{n} q_i b_i$ (the unique Hamel expansion), define:

$$f(x) = \sum_{i=1}^{n} q_i\, f(b_i).$$

This is well-defined because the Hamel expansion is unique.

**Step 3: $f$ satisfies Cauchy's equation.**

Let $x = \sum_i q_i b_i$ and $y = \sum_i r_i b_i$ (extending with zero coefficients so both use the same finite set of basis elements). Then

$$x + y = \sum_i (q_i + r_i) b_i,$$

which is the unique Hamel expansion of $x+y$. Therefore:

$$f(x+y) = \sum_i (q_i + r_i) f(b_i) = \sum_i q_i f(b_i) + \sum_i r_i f(b_i) = f(x) + f(y).$$

So $f$ is **additive**.

**Step 4: $f$ is discontinuous.**

Note that $f(b_0)=1$ and $f(b)=0$ for all $b\in B\setminus\{b_0\}$, so in particular $f$ is not identically zero. A standard result states:

> *If an additive function $f:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$ is Lebesgue measurable, or bounded on any set of positive measure, or continuous at even one point, then $f(x)=cx$ for some constant $c\in\mathbb{R}$.*

We show $f$ is **not** of the form $f(x)=cx$, hence it cannot be continuous.

Suppose for contradiction that $f(x)=cx$ for some $c$. Then $f(b_0)=1$ gives $c\, b_0 = 1$, so $c = 1/b_0$. But also, for any $b\in B\setminus\{b_0\}$, we have $f(b)=0$, which would require $c\, b = 0$, i.e., $b=0$. This contradicts the fact that basis elements are nonzero (and $B$ has more than one element, since $\mathbb{R}$ is uncountable-dimensional over $\mathbb{Q}$).

Therefore $f$ is not linear, hence not continuous.

**Step 5: $B$ has more than one element.**

$\mathbb{R}$ is uncountable while any finite-dimensional vector space over the countable field $\mathbb{Q}$ is countable. Hence $\dim_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{R}$ is infinite (in fact uncountable), so $B$ contains at least two distinct elements, confirming the argument above.

### Conclusion

Using a Hamel basis of $\mathbb{R}$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ (whose existence relies on the Axiom of Choice), we constructed an additive function $f$ that is not of the form $f(x)=cx$, and therefore is **discontinuous**. Such functions are necessarily non-measurable and cannot be explicitly described without some form of choice.

### PROOF COMPLETE
